{"item_id": "feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-sleeping-pad-inflators", "version_id": "678284250968c73e7b8f391aedae91c9d1fddc3fdad6b309f0fd685c1e536b86", "decision": "defer", "reason": "In scope under Relevance (sleep systems and power/charging gear), but the item rests on one reviewer's 1.5-year hands-on testing plus the publisher's own lab measurements, with no independent publisher or Mammoth-practitioner corroboration. Under Credibility and Uncertainty, the central argument needs at least three independent publishers, and the Affiliate model and Alpenglow/FLEXTAIL manufacturer specs (e.g., 43 dB, weights) are not independently confirmed here, so the evidence is too thin to rely on yet. Mammoth-specific relevance is also not established.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-winter-boots", "version_id": "85df28e1e6b983751fc364c96f8060a9d025a6fe52989cc6bed14e98cb8e0d5c", "decision": "reject", "reason": "Under Relevance, winter boots for town, casual, and work use fall outside the listed in-scope categories, which cover clothing and protection but not footwear for winter urban or work wear; several picks are for city and farm use, not multi-day carried-on-foot competition. The item is also a sales-adjacent affiliate guide with a sponsor-free but affiliate-driven structure and heavy reliance on the author's personal anecdotes and a family member's boots rather than Mammoth-relevant field testing. Under Suitability, nothing supports that these boots would be good or effective at Mammoth.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/exped-terra-sleeping-bag-review", "version_id": "9b22e426245124bef28813636a999eca8b200950b63c6e6ccd0028b7a03f4d41", "decision": "defer", "reason": "A sleeping bag is in scope under Relevance (sleep systems), and the review contains substantive field testing (nine months, Sierra at 8,000 ft, side-by-side comparisons, a measured 9 L compressed size), but the core evidence comes from one publisher with an affiliate model, and the 15°F warmth claim rests on testers' subjective impressions above freezing rather than measured lab results. Under Credibility and Commercial incentives, the manufacturer's temperature ratings are accepted only as claimed values, and the review itself notes its own tester sleeps cold while the ratings are unverified below freezing. Its Mammoth relevance is also not established, and it lacks three independent publishers, so it should be deferred until stronger evidence is available.", "piece_ids": []}
